  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1114 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nEvolving large scale pattern change will result in a deep trough \nover the western U.S. by the beginning of the week. Increasing \nmoisture will occur Sunday night into Monday morning so that by 12Z \nMonday deep layer RH will be >70% over the southern panhandle \nregion. Southwesterly flow aloft and short wave ripples in the flow \non Monday look to stay more in NM...although there is the \npotential for some strong winds over 40 mph mainly in the \nnorthwestern panhandles. The southwest U.S. shortwave will begin \nejecting out of the southwest and into the central plains late \nTuesday and into Wednesday with an increasing negative tilt and \ndeveloping speed max on the south side. This looks like it will \nbrush by our northwest area at that time so the best pcpn chances \nwill be Tuesday evening and then diminishing on Wed. At this time \ndue to the positioning of the ejecting trough it doesn't look \nlike any heavy rain amounts are expected then. However, the GFS \nmodel is hinting at better rain chances and heavier pcpn over \nportions of western TX thru western OK Friday into Saturday. Will \nneed to monitor the evolution of this during the upcoming week as \nthere is potential for heavy rain amounts wherever the axis of \nheaviest rain sets up.",
